Don't abort() when a client resets the connection mid-response

TemplateWrite() treats any write error other than EPIPE as fatal and calls
abort(), taking the whole server down. A client that closes while the
response is still being written produces EPIPE or, when its close races our
write, ECONNRESET. Both mean the peer is gone; neither means our output is
invalid, which is what the abort() guards against.

Kubernetes HTTP probes read at most 10 KiB of body and then close. The index
page is ~12 KiB, so every probe hangs up mid-response, and every so often the
race lands on ECONNRESET: the ~49-minute SIGABRT cycle (boettiger-lab/k8s#51).
A single client that resets mid-response reproduces it immediately.

diff --git a/src/util/Template.c b/src/util/Template.c
index 7a4f882..c3e9459 100644
--- a/src/util/Template.c
+++ b/src/util/Template.c
@@ -124,10 +124,13 @@ int TemplateWrite(TemplateRef const t, TemplateVarFn const var, void *const actx
 	for(size_t i = 0; i < t->count; i++) {
 		TemplateStep const *const s = &t->steps[i];
 		rc = wr(wctx, uv_buf_init((char *)s->str, s->len));
-		if(UV_EPIPE == rc) rc = 0;
+		// A client that hangs up mid-response surfaces as EPIPE or,
+		// if its close raced our write, ECONNRESET. Both mean the peer
+		// is gone, not that our output is wrong; treat them the same.
+		if(UV_EPIPE == rc || UV_ECONNRESET == rc) rc = 0;
 		if(rc < 0) abort();
 		rc = s->var && var ? var(actx, s->var, wr, wctx) : 0;
-		if(UV_EPIPE == rc) rc = 0;
+		if(UV_EPIPE == rc || UV_ECONNRESET == rc) rc = 0;
 		if(rc < 0) abort();
 		// Security critical!
 		// If we stop early but the caller ignores our error,
